1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,050
Can you show me from Docker to Kubernetes?

2
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:12,967
Docker runs containers on one machine, when you tell it to.

3
00:00:12,967 --> 00:00:18,888
Kubernetes runs the same images on a cluster of machines and keeps them running:

4
00:00:18,888 --> 00:00:23,773
you describe the desired state in YAML ("two copies of go-api:1.0,

5
00:00:23,773 --> 00:00:28,140
64 MiB each, reachable as go-api"), and Kubernetes creates,

6
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:32,210
restarts, replaces and spreads the containers to match.

7
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:39,019
Almost every docker run option you learned has a direct equivalent in a Kubernetes manifest.

8
00:00:40,767 --> 00:00:44,726
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

9
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:51,214
kubectl can write a manifest for you without a cluster (--dry-run=client).

10
00:00:51,214 --> 00:00:56,116
Ask for the Kubernetes version of a docker run of the Go API:

11
00:00:56,116 --> 00:00:58,688
Read it against the table above:

12
00:00:58,688 --> 00:00:59,411
replicas:

13
00:00:59,411 --> 00:01:04,715
2 (two containers, where Docker would need two docker run), image:

14
00:01:04,715 --> 00:01:06,804
go-api:1.0, containerPort:

15
00:01:06,804 --> 00:01:07,206
8080.

16
00:01:08,967 --> 00:01:10,973
Let me try something.

17
00:01:10,973 --> 00:01:13,266
There is no cluster yet.

18
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:18,428
kubectl is only a client, like docker (lesson 004).

19
00:01:18,428 --> 00:01:28,318
It reads the cluster address and credentials from its kubeconfig (~/.kube/config, or the file in KUBECONFIG).

20
00:01:28,318 --> 00:01:35,849
With no cluster configured, it falls back to localhost:8080, where nothing listens.

21
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:38,740
Create a cluster.

22
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:43,300
kind starts a Docker container that runs a complete Kubernetes node,

23
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:50,274
then writes a context kind-docker-course into the kubeconfig (this takes about a minute the first time):

24
00:01:52,033 --> 00:01:52,747
Got it.

25
00:01:52,747 --> 00:01:55,498
What do I need to remember?

26
00:01:56,033 --> 00:02:03,308
Kubernetes runs the same OCI images as Docker, on many machines, and keeps the desired state.

27
00:02:03,867 --> 00:02:08,209
docker run options map to Pod spec fields:

28
00:02:08,209 --> 00:02:13,791
image, env, ports, resources, probes, securityContext.

29
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:16,492
-p has no direct equivalent:

30
00:02:16,492 --> 00:02:21,503
Services give Pods a stable address; port-forward is for testing.

31
00:02:23,267 --> 00:02:27,032
I keep hearing about deploy an image to kind.

32
00:02:27,032 --> 00:02:28,621
What is it, really?

33
00:02:29,167 --> 00:02:33,753
The complete path from source code to a running Kubernetes workload:

34
00:02:33,753 --> 00:02:39,149
build the Go API's image with Docker (the multi-stage Dockerfile of lesson 089),

35
00:02:39,149 --> 00:02:45,422
load it into a kind cluster (in place of pushing it to a registry the cluster can pull from),

36
00:02:45,422 --> 00:02:50,953
deploy it with a Deployment and a Service, and reach it with kubectl port-forward.

37
00:02:50,953 --> 00:02:53,314
Then the most common first failure:

38
00:02:53,314 --> 00:02:57,226
a Pod stuck in ImagePullBackOff because of the latest tag.

39
00:02:58,967 --> 00:03:02,926
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

40
00:03:03,467 --> 00:03:03,655
1.

41
00:03:03,655 --> 00:03:08,088
Build the image, exactly as without Kubernetes:

42
00:03:08,088 --> 00:03:08,276
2.

43
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:09,006
2.

44
00:03:09,006 --> 00:03:11,168
Load it into the cluster.

45
00:03:11,733 --> 00:03:11,934
3.

46
00:03:11,934 --> 00:03:17,263
Deploy the manifests (deployment.yaml, service.yaml):

47
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,539
Let me try something.

48
00:03:20,539 --> 00:03:27,500
A colleague deploys the same image, but tagged latest, which they also loaded into the cluster:

49
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:36,353
kubectl describe shows a Pod's events, the Kubernetes equivalent of reading docker events and the error of docker run together:

50
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,943
Deploy an immutable version tag (the right fix; forcing imagePullPolicy:

51
00:03:42,943 --> 00:03:46,441
IfNotPresent on latest would only hide the problem).

52
00:03:46,441 --> 00:03:50,478
The pull policy is filled in when the Deployment is created,

53
00:03:50,478 --> 00:03:56,465
so kubectl set image alone would keep Always, and the kubelet would still try Docker Hub.

54
00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:58,913
Got it.

55
00:03:58,913 --> 00:04:01,665
What do I need to remember?

56
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:09,500
Build with Docker, load into kind (or push to a registry), deploy with a Deployment and a Service.

57
00:04:10,033 --> 00:04:14,988
kubectl port-forward reaches a Service from your computer for testing.

58
00:04:15,533 --> 00:04:16,978
ImagePullBackOff:

59
00:04:16,978 --> 00:04:21,993
the kubelet could not pull; kubectl describe pod shows why.

60
00:04:23,733 --> 00:04:27,098
How does kubernetes for Docker users work?

61
00:04:27,633 --> 00:04:33,168
Your Docker troubleshooting habits carry over to Kubernetes almost one to one:

62
00:04:33,168 --> 00:04:38,064
docker ps becomes kubectl get pods, docker logs becomes kubectl logs,

63
00:04:38,064 --> 00:04:45,656
docker inspect becomes kubectl describe, a throwaway docker run --rm on a network becomes kubectl run --rm.

64
00:04:45,656 --> 00:04:52,751
This lesson uses them on a running application, then debugs the most common Kubernetes-only failure:

65
00:04:52,751 --> 00:04:59,208
a readiness probe that never succeeds, which Kubernetes handles in a way Docker never does.

66
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:04,926
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

67
00:05:05,467 --> 00:05:11,376
docker ps → kubectl get pods, with the node and the Pod's own IP address:

68
00:05:11,376 --> 00:05:16,961
docker logs → kubectl logs, for every Pod of the application at once:

69
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:23,005
docker logs → kubectl logs, for every Pod of the application at once:

70
00:05:23,005 --> 00:05:28,111
docker run --rm on the application's network → kubectl run --rm.

71
00:05:28,111 --> 00:05:36,010
The Service name works as a DNS name, like a container name on a user-defined network (lesson 050):

72
00:05:36,567 --> 00:05:41,122
docker run --rm on the application's network → kubectl run --rm.

73
00:05:41,122 --> 00:05:48,170
The Service name works as a DNS name, like a container name on a user-defined network (lesson 050):

74
00:05:48,170 --> 00:05:51,586
sleep 2 gives kubectl time to attach to the Pod:

75
00:05:51,586 --> 00:05:59,274
a command that finishes faster than that can end before kubectl run -i is connected, and its output is lost.

76
00:05:59,274 --> 00:06:03,474
docker rm -f → kubectl delete pod, with one big difference:

77
00:06:03,474 --> 00:06:06,606
the Deployment immediately replaces the Pod.

78
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:10,180
Let me try something.

79
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:18,986
A colleague changes the readiness probe to port 9090 (the application listens on 8080) and applies it:

80
00:06:19,533 --> 00:06:22,055
The rollout never finishes.

81
00:06:22,055 --> 00:06:23,643
Look at the Pods:

82
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:33,471
Roll back to the last working version, then fix the manifest in Git (deployment.yaml still says 8080,

83
00:06:33,471 --> 00:06:34,750
which is right):

84
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:37,213
Got it.

85
00:06:37,213 --> 00:06:39,965
What do I need to remember?

86
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:45,743
get pods, logs, exec, describe, get events, run --rm:

87
00:06:45,743 --> 00:06:50,690
the Docker troubleshooting toolkit, in Kubernetes.

88
00:06:51,233 --> 00:06:54,207
Deleting a Pod is not like docker rm:

89
00:06:54,207 --> 00:06:56,378
its Deployment replaces it.

90
00:06:56,933 --> 00:07:02,444
A failing readiness probe keeps a Pod out of the Service and stops a rolling update:

91
00:07:02,444 --> 00:07:03,888
old Pods keep serving.
